{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01LUJ6xmrXojnBWhsrX6S9sn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. I wrote **`answers/04-005-fine-tune-or-prompt-engineered-rag.md`** — here's what's in it:\n\n---\n\n**Q5: Fine-tune or prompt-engineered RAG?**\n\nThe core diagnostic frame: **RAG closes knowledge gaps; fine-tuning closes behavior gaps** — and you must diagnose the root cause before choosing.\n\nKey content:\n\n- **Root-cause decision tree** — check RAGAS `context_recall` first; if recall@5 ≥ 0.85 but answers are still wrong in format/style, that's the signal for fine-tuning\n- **Use RAG when** data changes frequently, you need source attribution, you have < 1K labeled examples, or you're in early product stage\n- **Use fine-tune when** prompt iteration has genuinely failed (3–4 attempts), there's a schema/style/tone behavior gap, or inference cost/latency demand a smaller model\n- **Hybrid pattern** — fine-tune for *how to respond*; RAG for *what facts to use* — the production sweet spot for mature QA systems\n- **Concrete example** — customer support QA: retrieval recall@5 = 0.87 (good), but schema compliance 78% and disclaimer adherence 81%; LoRA fine-tune on 8K examples → 99%/100%, 4.5× cost reduction\n- **Three specific pitfalls** including the common error of reaching for fine-tuning before checking retrieval quality"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":307,"cache_read_input_tokens":71480,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":307,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62af15f4-1914-49ff-8bd1-9ba3f89acf9c","uuid":"95f80f81-c808-4c4b-ba63-09bb99911f7b"}
